00:11:23,840 --> 00:11:25,440
because a lot of people think corporate speak

255
00:11:25,440 --> 00:11:26,640
is just fluff.

256
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:27,520
Yeah, buzzwords.

257
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:29,540
They think it is people trying to sound smart

258
00:11:29,540 --> 00:11:31,540
or using buzz words to fill the air.

259
00:11:31,540 --> 00:11:32,800
And sure, sometimes it is,

260
00:11:32,800 --> 00:11:35,040
there is plenty of bad business English out there.

261
00:11:35,040 --> 00:11:35,880
Of course.

262
00:11:35,880 --> 00:11:37,540
But when you look at high functioning teams,

263
00:11:37,540 --> 00:11:39,200
like the ones in these transcripts,

264
00:11:39,200 --> 00:11:41,960
the language is actually a very sophisticated tool

265
00:11:41,960 --> 00:11:43,560
for anxiety reduction.

266
00:11:43,560 --> 00:11:45,800
Anxiety reduction, that's an interesting angle.

267
00:11:45,800 --> 00:11:46,800
I wanna pin that for later

268
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:48,300
because I think most people

269
00:11:48,300 --> 00:11:50,540
associate business meetings with anxiety,

270
00:11:50,540 --> 00:11:52,080
not the reduction of it.

271
00:11:52,080 --> 00:11:54,920
Exactly, and that is why this code exists,

272
00:11:54,920 --> 00:11:56,720
to manage that stress.

273
00:11:56,720 --> 00:11:57,900
Okay, let's start at the beginning,

274
00:11:57,900 --> 00:11:59,780
before the work even starts.

275
00:11:59,780 --> 00:12:01,740
Section one of our analysis

276
00:12:01,740 --> 00:12:04,780
draws from a few transcripts regarding preparation.

277
00:12:04,780 --> 00:12:09,860
And the overarching theme here seems to be alignment.

278
00:12:09,860 --> 00:12:12,300
Now alignment is one of those words I hear everywhere.

279
00:12:12,300 --> 00:12:13,140
It is ubiquitous.

280
00:12:13,140 --> 00:12:14,060
Are we aligned?

281
00:12:14,060 --> 00:12:15,360
Let's get aligned.

282
00:12:15,360 --> 00:12:17,380
We need better alignment.

283
00:12:17,380 --> 00:12:19,660
It is one of those buzz words that gets thrown around

284
00:12:19,660 --> 00:12:21,140
until it loses all meaning.

285
00:12:21,140 --> 00:12:21,980
Right.

286
00:12:21,980 --> 00:12:23,420
But when you look at the mechanics

287
00:12:23,420 --> 00:12:25,880
of these conversations in the source material,

288
00:12:25,880 --> 00:12:28,680
You see what it actually means mechanically.

289
00:12:28,680 --> 00:12:30,440
We have a source here where two colleagues

290
00:12:30,440 --> 00:12:33,680
are discussing a deck review before a client call.

291
00:12:33,680 --> 00:12:35,360
Now, if anyone hasn't spent time in a boardroom,

292
00:12:35,360 --> 00:12:38,320
a deck is just a presentation, usually PowerPoint.

293
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:40,480
Right, it's the visual aid for the narrative

294
00:12:40,480 --> 00:12:41,840
you're building, the slides.

295
00:12:41,840 --> 00:12:43,720
So one person asks for the deck and the other says,

296
00:12:43,720 --> 00:12:47,200
I will finalize two slides, verify with product,

297
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:48,360
standard stuff.

298
00:12:48,360 --> 00:12:50,680
But then there's this line that really stopped me.

299
00:12:50,680 --> 00:12:54,080
One speaker says, I want to avoid surprises during the Q&A.

300
00:12:54,080 --> 00:12:54,920
There it is.

301
00:12:54,920 --> 00:13:00,120
the other replies. We can also prepare backup examples in case they push back.

302
00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:05,800
That is the golden rule right there. Avoid surprises. If you are a listener trying to

303
00:13:05,800 --> 00:13:10,520
understand the mindset of a high-functioning workplace, or if you are learning business

304
00:13:10,520 --> 00:13:14,440
English to get a promotion, this is it. This is the core principle.

305
00:13:14,440 --> 00:13:17,560
So what's really happening there? In this context, they aren't worried about

306
00:13:17,560 --> 00:13:21,240
the content of the presentation as much as they are worried about the reaction to it.

307
00:13:21,240 --> 00:13:24,440
They're planning for the response. It's defensive driving, basically.

308
00:13:24,440 --> 00:13:24,940
It is.

309
00:13:24,940 --> 00:13:27,940
You're watching the other cars more than the road.

310
00:13:27,940 --> 00:13:28,440
It is.

311
00:13:28,440 --> 00:13:30,540
Think about what backup examples implies.

312
00:13:30,540 --> 00:13:33,240
They aren't putting those in the main presentation.

313
00:13:33,240 --> 00:13:34,640
They aren't cluttering the narrative.

314
00:13:34,640 --> 00:13:36,240
They're keeping them in their back pocket.

315
00:13:36,240 --> 00:13:38,740
Why not just put them in if they're good examples?

316
00:13:38,740 --> 00:13:41,440
Because it's an advanced communication strategy.

317
00:13:41,440 --> 00:13:43,740
You don't want to overwhelm the client with data.

318
00:13:43,740 --> 00:13:46,840
But if the client challenges you, if they push back,

319
00:13:46,840 --> 00:13:48,340
you need to be ready instantly.

320
00:13:48,340 --> 00:13:51,840
Because if you aren't, if they ask a tough question

321
00:13:51,840 --> 00:13:53,040
and you just stand there.

322
00:13:53,040 --> 00:13:56,640
If you aren't ready, you have to say, I don't know, or I'll have to get back to you.

323
00:13:56,640 --> 00:14:02,340
And in a high stakes meeting, that just... it kills momentum. It introduces doubt.

324
00:14:02,340 --> 00:14:03,700
It makes you look unprepared.

325
00:14:03,700 --> 00:14:08,100
Totally. But if you can say, I'm glad you asked, let me pull up this backup slide,

326
00:14:08,100 --> 00:14:11,460
you look incredibly competent. You look like you can predict the future.

327
00:14:11,460 --> 00:14:14,340
You look like you have already thought through their concerns.

328
00:14:14,340 --> 00:14:18,580
So that phrase, in case they push back, is really about anticipating conflict.

329
00:14:19,220 --> 00:14:20,980
Or at least friction.

330
00:14:20,980 --> 00:14:26,820
Precisely. Pushback is a very common business English idiom. It means resistance. It means the

331
00:14:26,820 --> 00:14:31,140
client disagrees or wants to argue. You don't say, in case they fight us.

332
00:14:31,140 --> 00:14:32,500
No, that sounds too aggressive.

333
00:14:32,500 --> 00:14:36,020
You say, in case they push back, it's softer, less emotional,

334
00:14:36,020 --> 00:14:40,020
but it describes the same thing. It depersonalizes the potential conflict.

335
00:14:40,020 --> 00:14:43,140
There's another word in that exchange I want to drill into. Smooth.

336
00:14:44,960 --> 00:14:50,840
They say, that will make the discussion smoother. Now in normal life, smooth is a texture.

337
00:14:50,840 --> 00:14:51,960
Right, peanut butter.

338
00:14:51,960 --> 00:14:54,210
It's peanut butter, it's a jazz song.

339
00:14:54,210 --> 00:14:59,330
But in business, smooth seems to mean something very specific.

340
00:14:59,330 --> 00:15:03,450
In this context, smooth means without friction.

341
00:15:03,450 --> 00:15:05,050
It means we don't want arguments,

342
00:15:05,050 --> 00:15:07,930
we don't want confusion, we don't want technical glitches.

343
00:15:07,930 --> 00:15:09,450
So it's about removing obstacles.

344
00:15:09,450 --> 00:15:12,530
When they say make the discussion smoother,

345
00:15:12,530 --> 00:15:15,570
they're talking about engineering the social interaction

346
00:15:15,570 --> 00:15:17,850
so that decision making happens easily.

347
00:15:17,850 --> 00:15:19,490
They're removing the speed bumps

348
00:15:19,490 --> 00:15:21,690
before the car even starts moving.

349
00:15:21,690 --> 00:15:24,490
Let's look at another example of this pregame engineering.

350
00:15:24,490 --> 00:15:27,370
We have a transcript here for a vendor call agenda.

351
00:15:27,370 --> 00:15:28,850
This is where they're setting up a meeting

352
00:15:28,850 --> 00:15:30,610
with an external company,

353
00:15:30,610 --> 00:15:32,130
and they're just drafting the agenda.

354
00:15:32,130 --> 00:15:34,530
This one is a masterclass in control.

355
00:15:34,530 --> 00:15:36,010
If you wanna know how to run a meeting,

356
00:15:36,010 --> 00:15:38,290
study this transcript, seriously.

357
00:15:38,290 --> 00:15:40,090
It really is, they use specific phrases.

358
00:15:40,090 --> 00:15:42,410
Decision point, buffer, recap.

359
00:15:42,410 --> 00:15:43,770
Let's unpack buffer.

360
00:15:43,770 --> 00:15:44,610
The source text says,

361
00:15:44,610 --> 00:15:47,690
can you also add a short buffer for questions at the end?

362
00:15:47,690 --> 00:15:50,890
And the response is, I will reserve five minutes.

363
00:15:50,890 --> 00:15:51,490
Buffer.

364
00:15:51,490 --> 00:15:53,890
It is such a utilitarian word.

365
00:15:53,890 --> 00:15:55,770
But think about what it acknowledges.

366
00:15:55,770 --> 00:15:56,290
Was it?

367
00:15:56,290 --> 00:15:58,810
It acknowledges that things go wrong.

368
00:15:58,810 --> 00:15:59,930
People talk too much.

369
00:15:59,930 --> 00:16:01,770
Technology fails.

370
00:16:01,770 --> 00:16:04,690
In a casual conversation, you just talk until you're done.

371
00:16:04,690 --> 00:16:07,690
In business English, time is a finite resource

372
00:16:07,690 --> 00:16:09,250
that you allocate like money.

373
00:16:09,250 --> 00:16:12,010
By calling it a buffer, they are proactively

374
00:16:12,010 --> 00:16:13,650
building in a safety net.

375
00:16:13,650 --> 00:16:15,290
It's admitting imperfection, isn't it?

376
00:16:15,290 --> 00:16:18,650
It's saying, we might run over, so let's plan for it.

377
00:16:18,650 --> 00:16:21,170
Exactly, and it protects your reputation.

378
00:16:21,170 --> 00:16:24,530
If you don't have a buffer and the meeting runs late,

379
00:16:24,530 --> 00:16:27,050
you look disorganized, you look unprofessional.

380
00:16:27,050 --> 00:16:28,530
You look like you don't respect their time.

381
00:16:28,530 --> 00:16:32,490
Precisely, but if you have a buffer and you end early,

382
00:16:32,490 --> 00:16:34,810
you are a hero, you gave them time back,

383
00:16:34,810 --> 00:16:36,130
everyone loves that.

384
00:16:36,130 --> 00:16:38,850
Then there's this phrase, decision point.

385
00:16:38,850 --> 00:16:42,050
Can you add a short decision point near the end?

386
00:16:42,050 --> 00:16:43,250
Why do they need to label it?

387
00:16:43,250 --> 00:16:44,970
Why not just decide?

388
00:16:44,970 --> 00:16:47,290
Because if you don't label it, people will just keep discussing.

389
00:16:47,290 --> 00:16:48,730
We've all been in those meetings, right?

390
00:16:48,730 --> 00:16:49,230
Oh, yes.

391
00:16:49,230 --> 00:16:51,410
Everyone talks for an hour, everyone nods,

392
00:16:51,410 --> 00:16:53,530
everyone feels good, and then everyone leaves.

393
00:16:53,530 --> 00:16:56,170
And you walk out and realize nobody actually

394
00:16:56,170 --> 00:16:57,330
agreed to do anything.

395
00:16:57,330 --> 00:16:58,610
The zombie meeting.

396
00:16:58,610 --> 00:17:01,010
It walks and talks, but it has no brain.

397
00:17:01,010 --> 00:17:02,650
The zombie meeting.

398
00:17:02,650 --> 00:17:05,130
It is alive, but dead.

399
00:17:05,130 --> 00:17:07,290
By putting decision point on the agenda,

400
00:17:07,290 --> 00:17:10,210
you are forcing the group to switch modes.

401
00:17:10,210 --> 00:17:12,930
You are signaling, OK, everyone, stop brainstorming,

402
00:17:12,930 --> 00:17:14,130
stop exploring.

403
00:17:14,130 --> 00:17:15,610
Now we are transacting.

404
00:17:15,610 --> 00:17:16,650
It's a different gear.

405
00:17:16,650 --> 00:17:17,490
It is.

406
00:17:17,490 --> 00:17:19,050
We are confirming pricing.

407
00:17:19,050 --> 00:17:20,570
We are confirming dates.

408
00:17:20,570 --> 00:17:22,850
It turns a conversation into a contract.

409
00:17:22,850 --> 00:17:24,490
It's a psychological trigger.

410
00:17:24,490 --> 00:17:25,530
Okay, everyone.

411
00:17:25,530 --> 00:17:28,450
Put your creative hats away and take out your checkbooks.

412
00:17:28,450 --> 00:17:29,290
That's it.

413
00:17:29,290 --> 00:17:30,610
It frames the conversation.

414
00:17:30,610 --> 00:17:33,330
And in Business English, framing is everything.

415
00:17:33,330 --> 00:17:34,570
You control the outcome

416
00:17:34,570 --> 00:17:36,730
by controlling the structure of the talk.

417
00:17:36,730 --> 00:17:38,570
I noticed one more small detail

418
00:17:38,570 --> 00:17:41,590
in that vendor call source, the tech check.

419
00:17:41,590 --> 00:17:42,430
One speaker says,

420
00:17:42,430 --> 00:17:44,910
I will open the call early and do a quick tech check.

421
00:17:44,910 --> 00:17:46,590
Ah, the tech check.

422
00:17:46,590 --> 00:17:49,070
It sounds so mundane, like obviously you check your mic.

423
00:17:49,070 --> 00:17:50,630
Why does this make it into the transcript?

424
00:17:50,630 --> 00:17:51,670
Why is it worth mentioning?

425
00:17:51,670 --> 00:17:53,430
Because it signals respect.

426
00:17:53,430 --> 00:17:55,950
It signals, I value your time enough

427
00:17:55,950 --> 00:17:58,230
to make sure my equipment works before you get here.

428
00:17:58,230 --> 00:17:59,830
So it's a professional courtesy.

429
00:17:59,830 --> 00:18:01,630
It is, but it also relates back

430
00:18:01,630 --> 00:18:03,590
to that smoothness we talked about.

431
00:18:03,590 --> 00:18:05,230
Technical issues are friction.

432
00:18:05,230 --> 00:18:07,430
A tech check is the grease,

433
00:18:07,430 --> 00:18:09,030
but there is a deeper layer

434
00:18:09,030 --> 00:18:11,150
in the remote meeting source we have.

435
00:18:11,150 --> 00:18:12,750
Oh, right, the backup plan.

436
00:18:12,750 --> 00:18:16,630
Yes, they discuss, if the video platform fails,

437
00:18:16,630 --> 00:18:19,230
we can switch to a conference line.

438
00:18:19,230 --> 00:18:21,070
That is redundancy.

439
00:18:21,070 --> 00:18:23,270
It is engineering for failure.

440
00:18:23,270 --> 00:18:25,670
It seems like pessimism is a secret ingredient

441
00:18:25,670 --> 00:18:27,310
of business English.

442
00:18:27,310 --> 00:18:28,990
You assume the tech will fail.

443
00:18:28,990 --> 00:18:30,950
You assume the client will push back.

444
00:18:30,950 --> 00:18:32,990
You assume the meeting will run long.

445
00:18:32,990 --> 00:18:34,110
I wouldn't call it pessimism.

446
00:18:34,110 --> 00:18:35,750
I would call it productive paranoia.

447
00:18:35,750 --> 00:18:37,550
Productive paranoia, I like that.

448
00:18:37,550 --> 00:18:39,390
You're paranoid so that you can be safe.

449
00:18:39,390 --> 00:18:40,390
Exactly.

450
00:18:40,390 --> 00:18:43,430
If you assume everything will go great, you are fragile.

451
00:18:43,430 --> 00:18:45,710
If you assume things will break, you are robust.

452
00:18:45,710 --> 00:18:47,990
The language reflects that robustness.

453
00:18:47,990 --> 00:18:50,630
Let's circle back to that first conversation about the deck,

454
00:18:50,630 --> 00:18:53,270
because I feel like we skimmed over something really important

455
00:18:53,270 --> 00:18:54,710
that connects to this paranoia.

456
00:18:54,710 --> 00:18:55,550
Okay.

457
00:18:55,550 --> 00:18:58,420
The source explicitly mentions,

458
00:18:58,420 --> 00:19:00,960
do you want the chart with notes or without?

459
00:19:00,960 --> 00:19:03,540
And the reply is, with notes would help

460
00:19:03,540 --> 00:19:06,100
since leadership asked about the assumptions.

461
00:19:06,100 --> 00:19:07,580
Oh, good catch.

462
00:19:07,580 --> 00:19:08,980
Assumptions.

463
00:19:08,980 --> 00:19:13,060
That is a massive word in financial and strategic business

464
00:19:13,060 --> 00:19:13,500
English.

465
00:19:13,500 --> 00:19:14,020
Right.

466
00:19:14,020 --> 00:19:17,720
Because in normal life, assuming is bad,

467
00:19:17,720 --> 00:19:19,320
don't make assumptions.

468
00:19:19,320 --> 00:19:21,040
We are taught that from childhood.

469
00:19:21,040 --> 00:19:23,520
But here, they're literally asking for them.

470
00:19:23,520 --> 00:19:26,640
In business, you never have 100% of the data.

471
00:19:26,640 --> 00:19:28,840
You are always guessing about the future.

472
00:19:28,840 --> 00:19:31,000
Assumptions are the educated guesses

473
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:32,480
you use to build your model.

474
00:19:32,480 --> 00:19:33,840
So they're the building blocks.

475
00:19:33,840 --> 00:19:34,400
They are.

476
00:19:34,400 --> 00:19:36,880
And by asking to list the assumptions,

477
00:19:36,880 --> 00:19:39,120
the leader is saying, show me your map.

478
00:19:39,120 --> 00:19:40,080
Show me the logic.

479
00:19:40,080 --> 00:19:41,860
It's a way of checking the foundation of the house

480
00:19:41,860 --> 00:19:42,920
before you buy it.

481
00:19:42,920 --> 00:19:45,520
Yes, if the logic is flawed, we can fix it.

482
00:19:45,520 --> 00:19:47,120
If you hide the assumptions

483
00:19:47,120 --> 00:19:49,280
and the numbers turn out to be wrong later,

484
00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:50,400
you look incompetent.

485
00:19:50,400 --> 00:19:52,120
You look like you didn't know what you were doing.

486
00:19:52,120 --> 00:19:53,680
Ah, so it's a form of protection.

487
00:19:53,680 --> 00:19:56,360
It is, but if you list the assumptions,

488
00:19:56,360 --> 00:19:57,960
we assumed 5% growth

489
00:19:57,960 --> 00:20:00,440
and the numbers are wrong because growth was only 2%,

490
00:20:00,440 --> 00:20:01,280
you're safe.

491
00:20:01,280 --> 00:20:03,040
You just look like you had bad data

492
00:20:03,040 --> 00:20:05,160
but your method was sound, it protects you.

493
00:20:05,160 --> 00:20:07,400
So listing assumptions is another form of mitigation.

494
00:20:07,400 --> 00:20:09,440
It mitigates the risk of being wrong.

495
00:20:09,440 --> 00:20:10,520
Precisely.

496
00:20:10,520 --> 00:20:14,200
It shifts the critique from the person to the logic.

497
00:20:14,200 --> 00:20:16,000
And that is a recurring theme.

498
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,360
Business English tries to take the target off the person's

499
00:20:18,360 --> 00:20:20,360
back and put it on the process.

500
00:20:20,360 --> 00:20:21,880
And in that same exchange, they

501
00:20:21,880 --> 00:20:24,480
talk about flagging risks early.

502
00:20:24,480 --> 00:20:26,880
Please flag any risks you notice early.

503
00:20:26,880 --> 00:20:29,280
Flagging, again, a visual metaphor,

504
00:20:29,280 --> 00:20:31,280
like putting a red flag in the ground

505
00:20:31,280 --> 00:20:32,840
or waving a flag on a battlefield.

506
00:20:32,840 --> 00:20:34,840
It means draw attention to this.

507
00:20:34,840 --> 00:20:36,840
You don't have to solve it yet, just flag it.

508
00:20:36,840 --> 00:20:38,760
So it's a request for, what?

509
00:20:38,760 --> 00:20:39,760
For visibility.

510
00:20:39,760 --> 00:20:41,600
Exactly, a request for visibility.

511
00:20:41,600 --> 00:20:43,920
I love how all these concepts interlock.

512
00:20:43,920 --> 00:20:46,080
You flag a risk to create visibility,

513
00:20:46,080 --> 00:20:47,920
which helps you align so you can mitigate

514
00:20:47,920 --> 00:20:49,160
and avoid surprises.

515
00:20:49,160 --> 00:20:51,680
It's a whole ecosystem of safety.

516
00:20:51,680 --> 00:20:54,600
I wanna go deeper into the vendor call agenda too.

517
00:20:54,600 --> 00:20:56,960
We talked about a buffer, but let's talk about the recap.

518
00:20:56,960 --> 00:20:59,760
The source says, include a quick recap of last week

519
00:20:59,760 --> 00:21:01,120
so they feel aligned.

520
00:21:01,120 --> 00:21:03,200
So they feel aligned, that is key.

521
00:21:03,200 --> 00:21:05,840
Alignment is a feeling as much as a fact.

522
00:21:05,840 --> 00:21:06,740
So it's emotional.

523
00:21:06,740 --> 00:21:07,580
It is.

524
00:21:07,580 --> 00:21:10,880
If the vendor feels lost, they will be defensive.

525
00:21:10,880 --> 00:21:13,740
They will ask questions you don't want them to ask.

526
00:21:13,740 --> 00:21:15,320
A recap anchors everyone.

527
00:21:15,320 --> 00:21:18,660
It says, here's where we were, here is where we are.

528
00:21:18,660 --> 00:21:20,920
It establishes a shared reality.

529
00:21:20,920 --> 00:21:24,650
It's like the previously on Second of a TV show.

530
00:21:24,650 --> 00:21:26,150
That's the perfect analogy.

531
00:21:26,150 --> 00:21:27,670
You can't start the new episode

532
00:21:27,670 --> 00:21:30,390
until you remember who got voted off the island last week.

533
00:21:30,390 --> 00:21:31,670
Exactly.

534
00:21:31,670 --> 00:21:33,990
And in business, people have short memories.

535
00:21:33,990 --> 00:21:36,110
They are juggling 10 projects.

536
00:21:36,110 --> 00:21:37,870
The recap is a courtesy that says,

537
00:21:37,870 --> 00:21:38,950
I know you are busy,

538
00:21:38,950 --> 00:21:41,150
let me remind you what we agreed on.

539
00:21:41,150 --> 00:21:42,550
It greases the wheels.

540
00:21:42,550 --> 00:21:44,790
And then the assignment of speakers.

541
00:21:44,790 --> 00:21:46,750
Assign the technical section to PREA

542
00:21:46,750 --> 00:21:48,470
and the budget to me.

543
00:21:48,470 --> 00:21:49,590
The vendor appreciates

544
00:21:49,590 --> 00:21:51,390
knowing who will lead each part.

545
00:21:51,390 --> 00:21:52,430
This is about authority.

546
00:21:52,430 --> 00:21:54,270
If you just show up and anyone talks,

547
00:21:54,270 --> 00:21:55,470
it looks like a mess.

548
00:21:55,470 --> 00:21:58,230
By assigning sections, you are designating experts.

549
00:21:58,230 --> 00:21:59,950
PREA is the technical lead.

550
00:21:59,950 --> 00:22:01,470
What is that signal to the vendor?

551
00:22:01,470 --> 00:22:04,430
It signals, if you have a tech question, ask Priya.

552
00:22:04,430 --> 00:22:05,690
Don't ask me.

553
00:22:05,690 --> 00:22:07,910
It channels the communication efficiently.

554
00:22:07,910 --> 00:22:09,550
It prevents that awkward silence

555
00:22:09,550 --> 00:22:10,710
where a question is asked

556
00:22:10,710 --> 00:22:12,650
and three people start talking at once.

557
00:22:12,650 --> 00:22:14,770
Or worse, nobody talks.

558
00:22:14,770 --> 00:22:18,550
Okay, so we have alignment, avoiding surprises,

559
00:22:18,550 --> 00:22:20,670
and engineering the meeting structure.

560
00:22:20,670 --> 00:22:23,070
That is all before the work even starts.

561
00:22:23,070 --> 00:22:24,670
Now, let's move to section two.

562
00:22:24,670 --> 00:22:25,730
Right.

563
00:22:25,730 --> 00:22:27,770
This is about when the work is actually happening.

564
00:22:27,770 --> 00:22:31,550
We're calling this visibility and managing workflow.

565
00:22:31,550 --> 00:22:34,110
Visibility is one of my favorite corporate concepts.

566
00:22:34,110 --> 00:22:35,770
It sounds like we're talking about eyesight,

567
00:22:35,770 --> 00:22:38,050
but we're really talking about that anxiety reduction

568
00:22:38,050 --> 00:22:38,890
I mentioned earlier.

569
00:22:38,890 --> 00:22:40,450
Explain that connection.

570
00:22:40,450 --> 00:22:42,890
How does visibility reduce anxiety?

571
00:22:42,890 --> 00:22:47,330
Well, imagine you hire a contractor to fix your kitchen,

572
00:22:47,330 --> 00:22:50,810
you pay them a deposit, and then silence.

573
00:22:50,810 --> 00:22:52,650
For three weeks, you don't see them,

574
00:22:52,650 --> 00:22:53,730
you don't hear from them.

575
00:22:53,730 --> 00:22:54,650
How do you feel?

576
00:22:54,650 --> 00:22:57,050
I feel like I've been scammed, I'm anxious,

577
00:22:57,050 --> 00:22:58,330
I'm checking my bank account.

578
00:22:58,330 --> 00:22:59,970
I'm calling them and they're not picking up.

579
00:22:59,970 --> 00:23:00,530
Exactly.

580
00:23:00,530 --> 00:23:02,290
Even if they are working hard in their workshop,

581
00:23:02,290 --> 00:23:02,810
you don't see it.

582
00:23:02,810 --> 00:23:04,010
You have no visibility.

583
00:23:04,010 --> 00:23:07,210
In a corporation, managers are the homeowners.

584
00:23:07,210 --> 00:23:09,570
They are terrified that work isn't happening.

585
00:23:09,570 --> 00:23:12,490
Visibility is the act of constantly showing your work

586
00:23:12,490 --> 00:23:13,490
so they don't panic.

587
00:23:13,490 --> 00:23:14,490
So it's reassurance.

588
00:23:14,490 --> 00:23:15,810
Constant reassurance.

589
00:23:15,810 --> 00:23:18,730
Let's look at the onboarding workflow source.

590
00:23:18,730 --> 00:23:21,330
They're drafting a document for new hires.

591
00:23:21,330 --> 00:23:24,250
They use words like polishing, circulate,

592
00:23:24,250 --> 00:23:26,450
accountability, and adoption.

593
00:23:26,450 --> 00:23:29,530
polishing. That is a great business English verb.

594
00:23:29,530 --> 00:23:34,410
One speaker says, we finish the first pass and are polishing the tone for clarity.

595
00:23:34,410 --> 00:23:39,010
Think about the signal that sends. If you tell your boss, I am writing it, what do

596
00:23:39,010 --> 00:23:40,010
they hear?

597
00:23:40,010 --> 00:23:41,210
Uh, that it's not done.

598
00:23:41,210 --> 00:23:45,730
They hear, I haven't finished. They might think you have barely started. It sounds

599
00:23:45,730 --> 00:23:50,170
heavy. It sounds like a lot of work is left. But if you say, I am polishing it, what

600
00:23:50,170 --> 00:23:51,170
do they hear?

601
00:23:51,170 --> 00:23:55,050
They hear it's done. It just needs a shine. It's almost ready to ship.

602
00:23:55,050 --> 00:24:01,210
done. It manages their expectations perfectly. It says, relax, the heavy lifting is over.

603
00:24:01,210 --> 00:24:02,970
It creates a sense of completion.

604
00:24:02,970 --> 00:24:07,070
Then it says, subtle but powerful distinction. Framing the stage of work. And then there's

605
00:24:07,070 --> 00:24:11,450
this discussion about the checklist. Please add a checklist section for managers. It keeps

606
00:24:11,450 --> 00:24:14,330
accountability clear without adding extra burden.

607
00:24:14,330 --> 00:24:17,850
That is the tightrope walk of management right there. You want accountability. You

608
00:24:17,850 --> 00:24:21,690
want to know people are doing their jobs, but you don't want to be a micromanager.

609
00:24:21,690 --> 00:24:23,690
You don't want to be a burden.

610
00:24:23,690 --> 00:24:25,850
So how does a checklist solve that?

611
00:24:25,850 --> 00:24:27,390
It seems like it is a burden.

612
00:24:27,390 --> 00:24:29,550
It depersonalizes the enforcement.

613
00:24:29,550 --> 00:24:32,170
If I come to your desk every day and say, did you do this?

614
00:24:32,170 --> 00:24:33,430
Did you do that?

615
00:24:33,430 --> 00:24:34,830
I'm annoying.

616
00:24:34,830 --> 00:24:35,830
I'm a burden.

617
00:24:35,830 --> 00:24:36,830
I am the bad guy.

618
00:24:36,830 --> 00:24:37,830
Right.

619
00:24:37,830 --> 00:24:38,830
I'd hate that.

620
00:24:38,830 --> 00:24:43,150
But if I give you a checklist, ideally the checklist is the bad guy.

621
00:24:43,150 --> 00:24:45,770
I'm just the helpful colleague giving you a tool.

622
00:24:45,770 --> 00:24:49,510
It shifts the authority from the person to the document.

623
00:24:49,510 --> 00:24:50,510
Oh.

624
00:24:50,510 --> 00:24:51,510
I'm not yelling at you.

625
00:24:51,510 --> 00:24:53,070
The checklist just says this needs to be done.

626
00:24:53,070 --> 00:24:56,350
and look at the word adoption in that same text.

627
00:24:56,350 --> 00:24:59,790
That tone fits our culture and encourages adoption.

628
00:24:59,790 --> 00:25:01,350
They aren't just writing a document

629
00:25:01,350 --> 00:25:02,430
for the sake of writing it.

630
00:25:02,430 --> 00:25:03,910
They want people to actually use it.

631
00:25:03,910 --> 00:25:05,230
They want adoption.

632
00:25:05,230 --> 00:25:07,950
They want the user to actually use the product.

633
00:25:07,950 --> 00:25:09,870
This shows a strategic mindset.

634
00:25:09,870 --> 00:25:11,310
It is not enough to deliver.

635
00:25:11,310 --> 00:25:13,590
The delivery has to work for the end user.

636
00:25:13,590 --> 00:25:16,670
Which leads us to the launch window crisis source.

637
00:25:16,670 --> 00:25:18,950
This is where things are not going smooth.

638
00:25:18,950 --> 00:25:20,710
They are at risk of missing a deadline.

639
00:25:20,710 --> 00:25:23,230
and you can feel the tension in the text.

640
00:25:23,230 --> 00:25:25,870
The launch window is kite, one environment keeps failing.

641
00:25:25,870 --> 00:25:29,390
This is a classic red status situation, high stress.

642
00:25:29,390 --> 00:25:32,550
But look at the language they use to handle that stress.

643
00:25:32,550 --> 00:25:35,630
They don't panic, they use very specific tools,

644
00:25:35,630 --> 00:25:39,950
surface blockers, visibility, trade off.

645
00:25:39,950 --> 00:25:41,510
Let's start with surface blockers.

646
00:25:41,510 --> 00:25:43,390
The source says, quick checkpoints

647
00:25:43,390 --> 00:25:45,350
help us surface blockers early.

648
00:25:45,350 --> 00:25:46,870
It's another visual metaphor.

649
00:25:46,870 --> 00:25:50,230
A blocker isn't just a problem, a problem is vague.

650
00:25:50,230 --> 00:25:51,670
I have a problem with the code.

651
00:25:51,670 --> 00:25:52,510
Okay, what does that mean?

652
00:25:52,510 --> 00:25:53,590
Are you stuck?

653
00:25:53,590 --> 00:25:54,430
Is it slow?

654
00:25:54,430 --> 00:25:55,510
It's not specific enough.

655
00:25:55,510 --> 00:25:56,350
No.

656
00:25:56,350 --> 00:25:58,510
A blocker implies a binary state.

657
00:25:58,510 --> 00:25:59,430
Movement is stopped.

658
00:25:59,430 --> 00:26:01,670
I cannot proceed until this is removed.

659
00:26:01,670 --> 00:26:02,590
It demands action.

660
00:26:02,590 --> 00:26:03,510
It screams for help.

661
00:26:03,510 --> 00:26:04,350
Yes.

662
00:26:04,350 --> 00:26:07,710
And surfacing, it means bringing it up from the deep.

663
00:26:07,710 --> 00:26:09,050
Think of a submarine.

664
00:26:09,050 --> 00:26:12,030
You need to surface the issue where everyone can see it.

665
00:26:12,030 --> 00:26:13,710
If you hide a problem in business,

666
00:26:13,710 --> 00:26:16,670
if you think, oh, I'll fix it before anyone notices,

667
00:26:16,670 --> 00:26:18,030
that is dangerous.

668
00:26:18,030 --> 00:26:19,590
Because if you can't fix it.

669
00:26:19,590 --> 00:26:21,730
If you fail, you fail alone.

670
00:26:21,730 --> 00:26:25,390
If you surface the blocker, the team owns the problem.

671
00:26:25,390 --> 00:26:26,870
That connects back to visibility.

672
00:26:26,870 --> 00:26:29,990
The source says visibility makes coordination smoother.

673
00:26:29,990 --> 00:26:33,110
In a crisis, silence is terrifying.

674
00:26:33,110 --> 00:26:34,310
If I don't know what you're doing,

675
00:26:34,310 --> 00:26:37,190
I assume the worst, I assume you're failing.

676
00:26:37,190 --> 00:26:40,230
Visibility means keeping everything in the open.

677
00:26:40,230 --> 00:26:43,830
The source mentions a shared board or daily checkpoint.

678
00:26:43,830 --> 00:26:45,750
I will set a daily checkpoint.

679
00:26:45,750 --> 00:26:48,630
Daily, that is high frequency.

680
00:26:48,630 --> 00:26:50,990
in peacetime you talk once a week.

681
00:26:50,990 --> 00:26:53,270
In wartime crisis time you talk every day.

682
00:26:53,270 --> 00:26:55,390
Daily checkpoint, it sounds intense.

683
00:26:55,390 --> 00:26:57,950
It is intense, but it prevents drift.

684
00:26:57,950 --> 00:27:00,670
If you wait a week, you might drift 10 miles off course.

685
00:27:00,670 --> 00:27:02,910
If you check in daily, you can correct the course

686
00:27:02,910 --> 00:27:04,270
when you're only one inch off.

687
00:27:04,270 --> 00:27:05,670
It keeps the team tight.

688
00:27:05,670 --> 00:27:07,030
And then when they realize

689
00:27:07,030 --> 00:27:08,910
they might actually miss the deadline,

690
00:27:08,910 --> 00:27:11,990
they don't just say we failed, they use the trade-off.

691
00:27:11,990 --> 00:27:13,190
This is brilliant.

692
00:27:13,190 --> 00:27:17,070
If testing slips, we may need to drop one minor feature.

693
00:27:17,070 --> 00:27:18,790
I will prepare a simple trade-off note.

694
00:27:18,790 --> 00:27:20,070
What is a trade-off note?

695
00:27:20,070 --> 00:27:21,310
It's a menu of pain.

696
00:27:21,310 --> 00:27:22,790
You're telling leadership, okay,

697
00:27:22,790 --> 00:27:24,990
we have a constraint, reality has hit us,

698
00:27:24,990 --> 00:27:27,230
we can either launch late with everything

699
00:27:27,230 --> 00:27:30,150
or launch on time with one less thing, you choose.

700
00:27:30,150 --> 00:27:32,310
It's just the agency back to the decision-maker.

701
00:27:32,310 --> 00:27:33,150
Exactly.

702
00:27:33,150 --> 00:27:34,750
You aren't presenting a failure,

703
00:27:34,750 --> 00:27:36,910
you're presenting a strategic choice.

704
00:27:36,910 --> 00:27:39,510
You look like a partner in the business,

705
00:27:39,510 --> 00:27:41,190
not a victim of circumstance.

706
00:27:41,190 --> 00:27:42,790
That is so much better than just saying,

707
00:27:42,790 --> 00:27:44,230
sorry, we're late.

708
00:27:44,230 --> 00:27:45,630
It's proactive.

709
00:27:45,630 --> 00:27:48,830
It frames the delay as a conscious decision,

710
00:27:48,830 --> 00:27:51,110
rather than an operational collapse.

711
00:27:51,110 --> 00:27:53,030
There is a phrase in the launch window crisis

712
00:27:53,030 --> 00:27:54,270
I wanna highlight.

713
00:27:54,270 --> 00:27:57,270
I will keep the shared board updated with the latest status,

714
00:27:57,270 --> 00:27:59,960
and the expert response in the source is,

715
00:27:59,960 --> 00:28:02,680
visibility makes coordination smoother.

716
00:28:02,680 --> 00:28:05,680
The shared board, whether it's Trello, Jira, Asana,

717
00:28:05,680 --> 00:28:07,320
whatever tool they are using,

718
00:28:07,320 --> 00:28:09,640
it is the single source of truth.

719
00:28:09,640 --> 00:28:11,680
Why is that so important in a crisis?

720
00:28:11,680 --> 00:28:13,200
In a crisis, rumor's sly.

721
00:28:13,200 --> 00:28:14,400
I heard testing failed.

722
00:28:14,400 --> 00:28:15,800
I heard we are delaying.

723
00:28:15,800 --> 00:28:17,440
The shared board kills rumors.

724
00:28:17,440 --> 00:28:18,600
It is the official record.

725
00:28:18,600 --> 00:28:19,520
It's the scoreboard.

726
00:28:19,520 --> 00:28:21,560
If it's not on the board, it isn't happening.

727
00:28:21,560 --> 00:28:25,080
It is, and updating it daily, as the source suggests,

728
00:28:25,080 --> 00:28:26,360
that is discipline.

729
00:28:26,360 --> 00:28:28,920
It tells the team, I am in control.

730
00:28:28,920 --> 00:28:31,640
Even if things are breaking, I am watching them break.

731
00:28:31,640 --> 00:28:32,520
Okay, let's keep moving.

732
00:28:32,520 --> 00:28:36,240
Section three, the vocabulary of risk and mitigation.

733
00:28:36,240 --> 00:28:37,310
Mm-hmm.

734
00:28:37,310 --> 00:28:38,910
This feels related to the crisis stuff,

735
00:28:38,910 --> 00:28:40,550
but this is more about looking ahead, right?

736
00:28:40,550 --> 00:28:41,390
Yes.

737
00:28:41,390 --> 00:28:43,870
Crisis management is when the house is on fire.

738
00:28:43,870 --> 00:28:46,790
Risk and mitigation is checking the smoke detectors.

739
00:28:46,790 --> 00:28:50,030
We have a source here from a steering committee risk review.

740
00:28:50,030 --> 00:28:51,030
Very formal.

741
00:28:51,030 --> 00:28:55,750
The key words here are mitigate, bandwidth, risk register,

742
00:28:55,750 --> 00:28:57,480
and cross-train.

743
00:28:57,480 --> 00:28:58,960
Let's talk about bandwidth.

744
00:28:58,960 --> 00:29:01,160
The source says, I will check their bandwidth.

745
00:29:01,160 --> 00:29:02,720
I know some people hate this word.

746
00:29:02,720 --> 00:29:05,320
They say it makes humans sound like internet cables

747
00:29:05,320 --> 00:29:06,520
or machines.

748
00:29:06,520 --> 00:29:07,800
It is a bit dehumanizing.

749
00:29:07,800 --> 00:29:09,160
Bandwidth is low.

750
00:29:09,160 --> 00:29:10,640
I always feel a bit silly saying it.

751
00:29:10,640 --> 00:29:11,360
It is.

752
00:29:11,360 --> 00:29:15,040
But in business English, it serves a very specific function.

753
00:29:15,040 --> 00:29:18,760
It turns human energy and time into a measurable quantity.

754
00:29:18,760 --> 00:29:22,400
You aren't asking, is he stressed or is he tired

755
00:29:22,400 --> 00:29:24,500
or is he going through a divorce?

756
00:29:24,500 --> 00:29:26,520
Which you probably shouldn't be asking anyway.

757
00:29:26,520 --> 00:29:29,200
Right, you're asking, does he have the capacity

758
00:29:29,200 --> 00:29:30,920
to take on more data?

759
00:29:30,920 --> 00:29:33,600
It removes the emotion from the workload discussion.

760
00:29:33,600 --> 00:29:34,840
It's just math.

761
00:29:34,840 --> 00:29:35,680
Exactly.

762
00:29:35,680 --> 00:29:37,800
If I say, I don't have the bandwidth,

763
00:29:37,800 --> 00:29:40,440
it's a professional way of saying, I cannot do this.

764
00:29:40,440 --> 00:29:42,880
It protects me, it's not a personal refusal,

765
00:29:42,880 --> 00:29:44,560
it's a resource limitation.

766
00:29:44,560 --> 00:29:48,520
It's easier to say than I'm overwhelmed or I'm too busy.

767
00:29:48,520 --> 00:29:49,480
It sounds objective.

768
00:29:49,480 --> 00:29:51,320
And then we have mitigate.

769
00:29:51,320 --> 00:29:54,160
Can we mitigate that by offering clearer specs?

770
00:29:54,160 --> 00:29:56,420
Mitigate is essential.

771
00:29:56,420 --> 00:29:59,000
You rarely solve risks in business completely.

772
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:01,360
You can't make them zero, the market might crash,

773
00:30:01,360 --> 00:30:03,760
the server might melt, people might quit.

774
00:30:03,760 --> 00:30:05,240
So you can only lessen the impact.

775
00:30:05,240 --> 00:30:06,600
You can only mitigate them,

776
00:30:06,600 --> 00:30:08,720
reduce their severity or likelihood.

777
00:30:08,720 --> 00:30:10,540
It's a word that shows you're realistic,

778
00:30:10,540 --> 00:30:12,380
you know things might go wrong,

779
00:30:12,380 --> 00:30:14,420
but you have a plan to soften the blow.

780
00:30:14,420 --> 00:30:16,620
And one of those plans mentioned in the source

781
00:30:16,620 --> 00:30:18,870
is to cross-train.

782
00:30:18,870 --> 00:30:21,190
Can we cross-crain someone from operations?

783
00:30:21,190 --> 00:30:23,550
This is a classic mitigation strategy.

784
00:30:23,550 --> 00:30:25,790
If only one person knows how to do the job,

785
00:30:25,790 --> 00:30:27,710
that is a single point of failure.

786
00:30:27,710 --> 00:30:28,990
Another great business phrase,

787
00:30:28,990 --> 00:30:30,830
though not in this specific text.

788
00:30:30,830 --> 00:30:32,230
What if that person gets sick?

789
00:30:32,230 --> 00:30:33,510
What if they quit?

790
00:30:33,510 --> 00:30:34,430
The bus factor.

791
00:30:34,430 --> 00:30:35,550
The bus factor.

792
00:30:35,550 --> 00:30:37,870
How many people have to get hit by a bus

793
00:30:37,870 --> 00:30:38,910
for the project to fail.

794
00:30:38,910 --> 00:30:40,870
Morbid, but accurate.

795
00:30:40,870 --> 00:30:43,190
Cross training increases the bus factor.

796
00:30:43,190 --> 00:30:44,830
It creates redundancy.

797
00:30:44,830 --> 00:30:47,730
I want to expand on the cross training discussion.

798
00:30:47,730 --> 00:30:50,620
The source connects it to bandwidth.

799
00:30:50,620 --> 00:30:53,680
I will check their bandwidth and discuss with their lead.

800
00:30:53,680 --> 00:30:56,360
This is the politics of resource sharing.

801
00:30:56,360 --> 00:30:58,940
You can't just steal an employee from another team.

802
00:30:58,940 --> 00:31:01,940
You have to check their bandwidth, do they have time,

803
00:31:01,940 --> 00:31:04,820
and discuss with their lead, ask permission.

804
00:31:04,820 --> 00:31:06,380
It respects the hierarchy.

805
00:31:06,380 --> 00:31:07,860
You don't want to step on toes.

806
00:31:07,860 --> 00:31:11,780
Yes, and Crosstrain implies a benefit for the employee too.

807
00:31:11,780 --> 00:31:15,140
It's not just do extra work, it's learn a new skill.

808
00:31:15,140 --> 00:31:16,620
We are going to Crosstrain you.

809
00:31:16,620 --> 00:31:18,540
It sounds like a development opportunity.

810
00:31:18,540 --> 00:31:20,820
Even if it's really just plugging a hole in the sinking ship.

811
00:31:20,820 --> 00:31:21,340
Right.

812
00:31:21,340 --> 00:31:23,340
That is the magic of business English.

813
00:31:23,340 --> 00:31:23,860
Right.

814
00:31:23,860 --> 00:31:26,580
Framing a staffing crisis as a learning opportunity.

815
00:31:26,580 --> 00:31:28,700
It's not cynical, it's efficient.

816
00:31:28,700 --> 00:31:31,420
It aligns the company's need, labor,

817
00:31:31,420 --> 00:31:33,860
with the employee's need, growth.

818
00:31:33,860 --> 00:31:36,180
Now there is a connection here to the risk register

819
00:31:36,180 --> 00:31:37,540
mentioned in the text.

820
00:31:37,540 --> 00:31:39,240
I will update the risk register.

821
00:31:39,240 --> 00:31:40,360
The risk register.

822
00:31:40,360 --> 00:31:42,420
It sounds like a dusty old book, but it's a shield.

823
00:31:42,420 --> 00:31:43,980
It's a paper trail.

824
00:31:43,980 --> 00:31:48,500
If a project fails because of a known risk, say, a vendor delay,

825
00:31:48,500 --> 00:31:51,140
and you didn't put it in the register, it's your fault.

826
00:31:51,140 --> 00:31:52,260
But if you wrote it down.

827
00:31:52,260 --> 00:31:54,740
But if you wrote it down three months ago,

828
00:31:54,740 --> 00:31:57,020
you can point to it and say, we knew this might happen.

829
00:31:57,020 --> 00:31:58,140
We planned for it.

830
00:31:58,140 --> 00:32:00,860
I told you so is not professional.

831
00:32:00,860 --> 00:32:01,900
It was in the risk register.

832
00:32:01,900 --> 00:32:03,100
It's very professional.

833
00:32:03,100 --> 00:32:04,100
Precisely.

834
00:32:04,100 --> 00:32:07,100
It turns a complaint into a record.

835
00:32:07,100 --> 00:32:09,860
Now, what about when the priorities shift entirely?

836
00:32:09,860 --> 00:32:12,260
We have a source here called Shifting Priorities,

837
00:32:12,260 --> 00:32:13,460
the Sprint.

838
00:32:13,460 --> 00:32:15,340
The market request escalates

839
00:32:15,340 --> 00:32:16,860
and they have to change the plan.

840
00:32:16,860 --> 00:32:19,620
This is the agile mindset in action.

841
00:32:19,620 --> 00:32:21,820
The keyword here is dependencies.

842
00:32:21,820 --> 00:32:24,820
The alert system depends on the new API.

843
00:32:24,820 --> 00:32:27,660
Identifying dependencies is how you explain delays

844
00:32:27,660 --> 00:32:29,000
without taking the blame,

845
00:32:29,000 --> 00:32:31,380
but also without throwing others under the bus.

846
00:32:31,380 --> 00:32:32,700
It's just a fact of the system.

847
00:32:32,700 --> 00:32:33,620
It's neutral.

848
00:32:33,620 --> 00:32:34,460
It is.

849
00:32:34,460 --> 00:32:36,180
We can't build the roof until the walls are up.

850
00:32:36,180 --> 00:32:38,300
The roof depends on the walls.

851
00:32:38,300 --> 00:32:40,940
It creates a logical chain of cause and effect.

852
00:32:40,940 --> 00:32:45,640
And when the dependency fails, the source mentions a fallback option.

853
00:32:45,640 --> 00:32:48,600
Let us identify a fallback option just in case.

854
00:32:48,600 --> 00:32:51,440
Plan B, but fallback sounds more technical, doesn't it?

855
00:32:51,440 --> 00:32:53,200
It sounds like a safety system.

856
00:32:53,200 --> 00:32:56,400
If the primary engine fails, the backup kicks in.

857
00:32:56,400 --> 00:32:57,800
It projects competence.

858
00:32:57,800 --> 00:33:01,880
It says, I have thought about failure and I have prepared for it.

859
00:33:01,880 --> 00:33:06,200
And throughout all of this, the source emphasizes transparency.

860
00:33:06,200 --> 00:33:09,540
Transparency helps avoid confusion and builds trust.

861
00:33:09,540 --> 00:33:13,560
Transparency is the currency of trust in remote or complex teams.

862
00:33:13,560 --> 00:33:17,640
If you hide the bad news, the shifting priorities, people get suspicious.

863
00:33:17,640 --> 00:33:21,020
If you are transparent, hey, the market changed, so we are changing.

864
00:33:21,020 --> 00:33:22,020
People align with you.

865
00:33:22,020 --> 00:33:23,020
They get it.

866
00:33:23,020 --> 00:33:26,780
So we have prepared, we're managing the work, we're mitigating risks, now we need

867
00:33:26,780 --> 00:33:28,780
to convince people that we're doing a good job.

868
00:33:28,780 --> 00:33:31,700
Section four, strategic decision-making and metrics.

869
00:33:31,700 --> 00:33:33,420
This is the quarterly review source.

870
00:33:33,420 --> 00:33:36,900
This is where you are selling your success to the client or the leadership.

871
00:33:36,900 --> 00:33:39,340
The vocabulary here is very forward-looking.

872
00:33:39,340 --> 00:33:41,780
Roadmap, phased expansion, tentative.

873
00:33:41,780 --> 00:33:42,980
Let's talk about the roadmap.

874
00:33:42,980 --> 00:33:44,500
The roadmap.

875
00:33:44,500 --> 00:33:47,140
It is the holy grail of business planning.

876
00:33:47,140 --> 00:33:49,380
A roadmap is not a calendar.

877
00:33:49,380 --> 00:33:52,340
A calendar says Tuesday at 2 p.m.

878
00:33:52,340 --> 00:33:56,620
A roadmap says, in Q3, we are going in this direction.

879
00:33:56,620 --> 00:33:59,500
It is about direction and intent.

880
00:33:59,500 --> 00:34:02,980
The source says, the client plans their budget around it.

881
00:34:02,980 --> 00:34:04,680
It shows the weight of this document.

882
00:34:04,680 --> 00:34:06,480
It connects language to money.

883
00:34:06,480 --> 00:34:09,120
That is why business English is so precise here.

884
00:34:09,120 --> 00:34:16,480
If you say, we will launch in Q3, the client sets aside $100,000 for marketing in Q3.

885
00:34:16,480 --> 00:34:18,940
If you're late, they wasted that money.

886
00:34:18,940 --> 00:34:20,980
Your words have financial consequences.

887
00:34:20,980 --> 00:34:23,040
Which is why they also use the word tentative.

888
00:34:23,040 --> 00:34:26,400
I will verify with product and mark anything still tentative.

889
00:34:26,400 --> 00:34:27,800
Tentative is your legal defense.

890
00:34:27,800 --> 00:34:31,160
It allows you to put something on the roadmap without signing in blood.

891
00:34:31,160 --> 00:34:35,640
signals we want to do this but don't fire me if it doesn't happen. It manages the risk of

892
00:34:35,640 --> 00:34:40,680
over-promising. So when they say mark anything still tentative, they are literally saving the

893
00:34:40,680 --> 00:34:45,880
client money. And saving themselves from a lawsuit. Tentative means do not spend money on this yet.

894
00:34:45,880 --> 00:34:49,880
And when they're proposing an expansion, they don't just say let's do it all. They propose a

895
00:34:49,880 --> 00:34:55,480
phased expansion. I will propose a phased expansion starting with one region. This is

896
00:34:55,480 --> 00:35:02,120
classic risk reduction. Leaders get scared of big sudden changes. Big changes cost money

897
00:35:02,120 --> 00:35:03,120
and break things.

898
00:35:03,120 --> 00:35:05,920
It feels risky to say yes to a big change.

899
00:35:05,920 --> 00:35:11,000
Exactly. A phased approach feels safe. It's dipping a toe in a water. You can always stop

900
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:14,360
after phase one. It lowers the barrier to approval.

901
00:35:14,360 --> 00:35:16,760
It makes it easier for the boss to say yes.

902
00:35:16,760 --> 00:35:22,040
Yes. And notice the mention of the learning loop. Provides a learning loop before scaling.

903
00:35:22,040 --> 00:35:23,440
I love learning loop.

904
00:35:23,440 --> 00:35:25,760
It sounds so much better than trial and error.

905
00:35:25,760 --> 00:35:27,360
Trial and error sounds random.

906
00:35:27,360 --> 00:35:29,000
Learning loop sounds systematic.

907
00:35:29,000 --> 00:35:30,920
It frames failure as data.

908
00:35:30,920 --> 00:35:33,200
If the pilot project fails, it wasn't a failure.

909
00:35:33,200 --> 00:35:34,640
It was a learning loop.

910
00:35:34,640 --> 00:35:35,920
You got information out of it.

911
00:35:35,920 --> 00:35:37,360
We learned what doesn't work

912
00:35:37,360 --> 00:35:38,960
so we can do it better next time.

913
00:35:38,960 --> 00:35:40,760
It encourages experimentation

914
00:35:40,760 --> 00:35:43,000
without the fear of punishment.

915
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:47,070
And scaling, before scaling, you don't just grow,

916
00:35:47,070 --> 00:35:48,430
you scale.

917
00:35:48,430 --> 00:35:51,110
Scale implies growing without breaking.

918
00:35:51,110 --> 00:35:52,230
It implies structure.

919
00:35:52,230 --> 00:35:53,070
That's right.

920
00:35:53,070 --> 00:35:55,630
And to get that approval, you need a success metric.

921
00:35:55,630 --> 00:35:57,990
We need a clear success metric for expansion.

922
00:35:57,990 --> 00:35:59,870
You can't just say, it is going well.

923
00:35:59,870 --> 00:36:01,390
That is an opinion.

924
00:36:01,390 --> 00:36:03,590
Success metric means data.

925
00:36:03,590 --> 00:36:06,270
Retention scores, satisfaction scores.

926
00:36:06,270 --> 00:36:09,070
It turns an opinion into a fact.

927
00:36:09,070 --> 00:36:12,190
In business English, facts win arguments.

928
00:36:12,190 --> 00:36:13,270
Okay, let's pivot.

929
00:36:13,270 --> 00:36:15,430
We've talked a lot about the hard schools metrics,

930
00:36:15,430 --> 00:36:17,470
roadmaps, risk.

931
00:36:17,470 --> 00:36:19,790
But business is done by people.

932
00:36:19,790 --> 00:36:23,150
Section five is about relationship management and soft skills.

933
00:36:23,150 --> 00:36:25,030
Soft skills, but hard strategy.

934
00:36:25,030 --> 00:36:26,590
The sources here are about feedback,

935
00:36:26,590 --> 00:36:28,270
tension, and transitions.

936
00:36:28,270 --> 00:36:30,950
Let's look at the support team feedback source.

937
00:36:30,950 --> 00:36:32,990
The support team is unhappy.

938
00:36:32,990 --> 00:36:34,390
They are complaining.

939
00:36:34,390 --> 00:36:37,150
The vocabulary used to fix it is fascinating.

940
00:36:37,150 --> 00:36:39,190
Quick wins, acknowledge the pain.

941
00:36:39,190 --> 00:36:41,790
Quick wins is a psychological tactic.

942
00:36:41,790 --> 00:36:44,030
The source says, quick wins show momentum

943
00:36:44,030 --> 00:36:45,870
and build trust fast.

944
00:36:45,870 --> 00:36:47,590
If you have a big long-term project,

945
00:36:47,590 --> 00:36:49,710
say a two-year software overhaul,

946
00:36:49,710 --> 00:36:51,570
People lose faith, they get bored,

947
00:36:51,570 --> 00:36:52,670
they think nothing is happening.

948
00:36:52,670 --> 00:36:53,790
So you give them a quick win.

949
00:36:53,790 --> 00:36:56,050
You fix something small and annoying immediately,

950
00:36:56,050 --> 00:36:58,190
you deliver something, it proves you're active,

951
00:36:58,190 --> 00:37:00,050
it buys you patience for the long haul.

952
00:37:00,050 --> 00:37:01,390
And acknowledge the pain.

953
00:37:01,390 --> 00:37:02,950
That sounds almost like therapy.

954
00:37:02,950 --> 00:37:04,450
It does.

955
00:37:04,450 --> 00:37:07,930
We should acknowledge the pain and propose next steps.

956
00:37:07,930 --> 00:37:10,570
In a business context, pain usually means

957
00:37:10,570 --> 00:37:13,770
inefficiency or frustration, pain points.

958
00:37:13,770 --> 00:37:16,850
By acknowledging it, you are validating their experience.

959
00:37:16,850 --> 00:37:18,530
You're saying, I see you are struggling

960
00:37:18,530 --> 00:37:19,990
and I am not ignoring it.

961
00:37:19,990 --> 00:37:22,930
If you skip that step, if you just jump to the fit.

962
00:37:22,930 --> 00:37:25,490
If you skip that step and just jump to the solution,

963
00:37:25,490 --> 00:37:27,050
people feel unheard.

964
00:37:27,050 --> 00:37:28,090
They resist the solution.

965
00:37:28,090 --> 00:37:30,010
You have to build the emotional bridge first.

966
00:37:30,010 --> 00:37:32,250
Now what if the meeting goes badly?

967
00:37:32,250 --> 00:37:34,050
We have a source called the reset meeting.

968
00:37:34,050 --> 00:37:35,610
There was tension in the last call.

969
00:37:35,610 --> 00:37:36,810
I love the word reset.

970
00:37:36,810 --> 00:37:37,810
We need a reset.

971
00:37:37,810 --> 00:37:39,370
It wipes the slate clean.

972
00:37:39,370 --> 00:37:41,610
It's not an apology exactly, it's a recalibration.

973
00:37:41,610 --> 00:37:43,450
It says, last time didn't work,

974
00:37:43,450 --> 00:37:45,010
let's try a different mode.

975
00:37:45,010 --> 00:37:46,650
And to make that reset work,

976
00:37:46,650 --> 00:37:48,610
They propose ground rules.

977
00:37:48,610 --> 00:37:50,550
I can propose short speaking turns

978
00:37:50,550 --> 00:37:52,250
and a visible agenda timer.

979
00:37:52,250 --> 00:37:53,090
Ground rules?

980
00:37:53,090 --> 00:37:54,730
It sounds like kindergarten.

981
00:37:54,730 --> 00:37:58,210
It does, but it works because it depersonalizes the conflict.

982
00:37:58,210 --> 00:38:00,090
Instead of saying Dave, stop interrupting,

983
00:38:00,090 --> 00:38:02,930
you say, we have a ground rule about turn taking.

984
00:38:02,930 --> 00:38:06,650
Ah, so it makes the behavior about the rule, not the person.

985
00:38:06,650 --> 00:38:07,370
Exactly.

986
00:38:07,370 --> 00:38:10,170
It saves face for Dave, but it solves the problem.

987
00:38:10,170 --> 00:38:13,110
And finally, in this section, the handoff.

988
00:38:13,110 --> 00:38:15,630
Moving an account to a new manager.

989
00:38:15,630 --> 00:38:17,970
Transition Dock. Open items.

990
00:38:17,970 --> 00:38:19,430
Open items is crucial here.

991
00:38:19,430 --> 00:38:21,310
Things that aren't finished yet.

992
00:38:21,310 --> 00:38:23,270
You never want to hand off a project

993
00:38:23,270 --> 00:38:24,830
and pretend it is perfect.

994
00:38:24,830 --> 00:38:27,750
You list the open items so the new person knows exactly

995
00:38:27,750 --> 00:38:28,830
where the landmines are.

996
00:38:28,830 --> 00:38:29,870
It's about honesty.

997
00:38:29,870 --> 00:38:31,590
It is. It protects the relationship

998
00:38:31,590 --> 00:38:34,230
because the new person isn't blindsided.

999
00:38:34,230 --> 00:38:35,350
In the soft skills section,

1000
00:38:35,350 --> 00:38:37,510
the handoff conversation mentions,

1001
00:38:37,510 --> 00:38:39,150
ensure the new manager understands

1002
00:38:39,150 --> 00:38:41,030
the renewal timeline clearly.

1003
00:38:41,030 --> 00:38:42,510
The renewal timeline.

1004
00:38:42,510 --> 00:38:44,750
That is the lifeblood of the business.

1005
00:38:44,750 --> 00:38:46,270
When does the contract end?

1006
00:38:46,270 --> 00:38:47,750
When do we get paid again?

1007
00:38:47,750 --> 00:38:49,830
Passing that information is critical.

1008
00:38:49,830 --> 00:38:52,110
If the new manager misses the renewal date,

1009
00:38:52,110 --> 00:38:53,470
the company loses revenue.

1010
00:38:53,470 --> 00:38:56,270
So the transition doc isn't just a list of names.

1011
00:38:56,270 --> 00:38:57,830
It's a map of the revenue.

1012
00:38:57,830 --> 00:38:58,670
Exactly.

1013
00:38:58,670 --> 00:39:01,270
And the source asks, do we need a live handoff call

1014
00:39:01,270 --> 00:39:02,590
or is the document enough?

1015
00:39:02,590 --> 00:39:03,750
And they decide on the call.

1016
00:39:03,750 --> 00:39:04,670
Why?

1017
00:39:04,670 --> 00:39:05,750
Why not just send the email?

1018
00:39:05,750 --> 00:39:06,710
It's more efficient.

1019
00:39:06,710 --> 00:39:09,190
Because documents convey facts,

1020
00:39:09,190 --> 00:39:11,310
but calls convey trust.

1021
00:39:11,310 --> 00:39:14,110
You can't hand off a relationship in a PDF.

1022
00:39:14,110 --> 00:39:16,350
The client needs to see the two managers together,

1023
00:39:16,350 --> 00:39:18,710
see them smiling, see them aligned.

1024
00:39:18,710 --> 00:39:21,110
It transfers the trust from the old manager

1025
00:39:21,110 --> 00:39:21,950
to the new one.

1026
00:39:21,950 --> 00:39:23,770
It's ceremonial passing of the torch.

1027
00:39:23,770 --> 00:39:24,590
It is.

1028
00:39:24,590 --> 00:39:26,990
A smooth handoff creates continuity.

1029
00:39:26,990 --> 00:39:28,590
The client shouldn't feel a bump.

1030
00:39:28,590 --> 00:39:30,590
All right, we are in the home stretch.

1031
00:39:30,590 --> 00:39:33,390
Section six, action-oriented closings.

1032
00:39:33,390 --> 00:39:34,230
No.

1033
00:39:34,230 --> 00:39:35,990
You've had the meeting, you have aligned,

1034
00:39:35,990 --> 00:39:37,830
you have mitigated, how do you end it?

1035
00:39:37,830 --> 00:39:40,670
You end it with action items and owners.

1036
00:39:40,670 --> 00:39:42,110
The source is very specific here.

1037
00:39:42,110 --> 00:39:43,390
I will outline the owners.

1038
00:39:43,390 --> 00:39:44,970
Active ownership is the goal.

1039
00:39:44,970 --> 00:39:47,210
Passive voice is the enemy of business.

1040
00:39:47,210 --> 00:39:48,330
It will get done.

1041
00:39:48,330 --> 00:39:49,370
By whom? When?

1042
00:39:49,370 --> 00:39:51,050
By whom? When?

1043
00:39:51,050 --> 00:39:52,730
That is how tasks die.

1044
00:39:52,730 --> 00:39:53,930
The bystander effect.

1045
00:39:53,930 --> 00:39:54,770
Exactly.

1046
00:39:54,770 --> 00:39:56,290
If you don't assign an owner,

1047
00:39:56,290 --> 00:39:57,730
everyone thinks someone else is doing it.

1048
00:39:57,730 --> 00:40:00,410
An owner is the single person whose neck is on the line.

1049
00:40:00,410 --> 00:40:03,050
It sounds harsh, but it creates clarity.

1050
00:40:03,050 --> 00:40:03,930
Priya will do it.

1051
00:40:03,930 --> 00:40:04,930
Now Priya knows.

1052
00:40:04,930 --> 00:40:06,330
Now everyone knows.

1053
00:40:06,330 --> 00:40:08,730
And there's this beautiful phrase in the follow-up source.

1054
00:40:08,730 --> 00:40:10,010
Queue it for you.

1055
00:40:10,010 --> 00:40:12,130
I will draft it and queue it for you.

1056
00:40:12,130 --> 00:40:14,410
That is the ultimate service-oriented phrase.

1057
00:40:14,410 --> 00:40:15,250
It's one of my favorites.

1058
00:40:15,250 --> 00:40:16,250
What does it actually mean?

1059
00:40:16,250 --> 00:40:19,450
It means I will do 99% of the work.

1060
00:40:19,450 --> 00:40:21,370
I will write the email, attach the file,

1061
00:40:21,370 --> 00:40:22,210
put in the address.

1062
00:40:22,210 --> 00:40:24,010
All you have to do is press send.

1063
00:40:24,010 --> 00:40:27,410
It is about removing friction for your boss or your client.

1064
00:40:27,410 --> 00:40:28,610
It's managing up.

1065
00:40:28,610 --> 00:40:29,370
It is.

1066
00:40:29,370 --> 00:40:31,090
If you ask your boss to write an email,

1067
00:40:31,090 --> 00:40:32,610
they might put it off for a week.

1068
00:40:32,610 --> 00:40:35,330
If you queue it up for them, they do it in 10 seconds.

1069
00:40:35,330 --> 00:40:36,690
You are buying speed.

1070
00:40:36,690 --> 00:40:38,810
You are making yourself indispensable.

1071
00:40:38,810 --> 00:40:40,210
It's about momentum.

1072
00:40:40,210 --> 00:40:42,790
The source mentions, quick wins show momentum.

1073
00:40:42,790 --> 00:40:45,010
Cue it for you, keeps the momentum going.

1074
00:40:45,010 --> 00:40:46,970
If I wait for you to write the email,

1075
00:40:46,970 --> 00:40:48,770
it might take three days.

1076
00:40:48,770 --> 00:40:51,490
If I write it for you, it takes three minutes.

1077
00:40:51,490 --> 00:40:54,210
And action items, the source says.

1078
00:40:54,210 --> 00:40:57,250
I will capture action items and confirm the next steps.

1079
00:40:57,250 --> 00:40:59,790
Capture, you aren't just writing them,

1080
00:40:59,790 --> 00:41:01,610
you're capturing them like wild animals

1081
00:41:01,610 --> 00:41:02,690
before they run away.

1082
00:41:02,690 --> 00:41:03,690
A great way to put it.

1083
00:41:03,690 --> 00:41:06,010
If you don't capture them in the meeting, they are gone.

1084
00:41:06,010 --> 00:41:07,850
And confirm, confirm with managers,

1085
00:41:07,850 --> 00:41:09,370
confirm the next meeting date.

1086
00:41:09,370 --> 00:41:11,290
Confirmation is the lock.

1087
00:41:11,290 --> 00:41:14,010
You haven't agreed until you have confirmed.

1088
00:41:14,010 --> 00:41:15,730
It closes the loop.

1089
00:41:15,730 --> 00:41:18,370
Another great phrase, close the loop.

1090
00:41:18,370 --> 00:41:19,810
There's endless depth here.

1091
00:41:19,810 --> 00:41:22,890
And notice how every single interaction in these sources

1092
00:41:22,890 --> 00:41:26,550
ends with a document, a summary email,

1093
00:41:26,550 --> 00:41:29,310
a calendar reminder, a checklist.

1094
00:41:29,310 --> 00:41:31,430
If it isn't written down, it didn't happen.

1095
00:41:31,430 --> 00:41:33,150
The conversation evaporates.

1096
00:41:33,150 --> 00:41:34,950
The document remains.

1097
00:41:34,950 --> 00:41:37,750
That is the final law of business English.

1098
00:41:37,750 --> 00:41:39,230
Send the recap.

1099
00:41:39,230 --> 00:41:40,790
It's the save game function.

1100
00:41:40,790 --> 00:41:41,630
Exactly.

1101
00:41:41,630 --> 00:41:43,190
You lock in the progress.

1102
00:41:43,190 --> 00:41:44,030
Before we wrap up,

1103
00:41:44,030 --> 00:41:46,310
I want to touch on the training plan source we skimmed.

1104
00:41:46,310 --> 00:41:47,630
I drafted a schedule.

1105
00:41:47,630 --> 00:41:50,270
Can we keep each session under 60 minutes?

1106
00:41:50,270 --> 00:41:51,590
Include role play time.

1107
00:41:51,590 --> 00:41:52,590
Role play.

1108
00:41:52,590 --> 00:41:53,990
People hate it, but it works.

1109
00:41:53,990 --> 00:41:55,670
It changes knowledge into skill.

1110
00:41:55,670 --> 00:41:57,350
You can read about active listening,

1111
00:41:57,350 --> 00:41:59,590
but until you role play it, you can't do it.

1112
00:41:59,590 --> 00:42:01,310
And facilitators.

1113
00:42:01,310 --> 00:42:02,310
They don't call them teachers.

1114
00:42:02,310 --> 00:42:03,950
No, a teacher delivers knowledge.

1115
00:42:03,950 --> 00:42:06,230
A facilitator makes the process easier.

1116
00:42:06,230 --> 00:42:07,910
They facilitate the learning.

1117
00:42:07,910 --> 00:42:10,710
It implies that the learners are active participants,

1118
00:42:10,710 --> 00:42:11,910
not just empty vessels.

1119
00:42:11,910 --> 00:42:14,210
And align the assessment with our standards.

1120
00:42:14,210 --> 00:42:16,230
Alignment again, it never goes away.

1121
00:42:16,230 --> 00:42:17,070
It's everywhere.

1122
00:42:17,070 --> 00:42:20,510
If the training doesn't match the job, the standards,

1123
00:42:20,510 --> 00:42:22,010
it is a waste of time.

1124
00:42:22,010 --> 00:42:23,870
Alignment ensures value.

1125
00:42:23,870 --> 00:42:26,510
It is the unifying theory of business English.

1126
00:42:26,510 --> 00:42:27,670
Everything must align.

1127
00:42:27,670 --> 00:42:28,830
Everything must align.

1128
00:42:28,830 --> 00:42:29,670
So let's zoom out.

1129
00:42:29,670 --> 00:42:31,430
We've gone through a lot of transcripts.

1130
00:42:31,430 --> 00:42:34,190
We've seen alignment, visibility, mitigation,

1131
00:42:34,190 --> 00:42:36,540
roadmaps, owners.

1132
00:42:36,540 --> 00:42:37,660
What's the big picture here?

1133
00:42:37,660 --> 00:42:39,500
What's the expert takeaway?

1134
00:42:39,500 --> 00:42:41,180
If you look at all these briefs together,

1135
00:42:41,180 --> 00:42:43,580
the overarching theme is predictability.

1136
00:42:43,580 --> 00:42:46,700
Business English is a language designed to tame the future.

1137
00:42:46,700 --> 00:42:47,900
The future is scary.

1138
00:42:47,900 --> 00:42:50,980
It has risks, it has delays, it has angry clients.

1139
00:42:50,980 --> 00:42:51,940
It is chaos.

1140
00:42:51,940 --> 00:42:53,300
It is chaos.

1141
00:42:53,300 --> 00:42:56,740
Words like a line, roadmap, buffer, and visibility

1142
00:42:56,740 --> 00:43:00,240
are all tools to make that scary future look predictable.

1143
00:43:00,240 --> 00:43:01,680
They are control mechanisms.

1144
00:43:01,680 --> 00:43:03,820
They turn the chaos into a process.

1145
00:43:03,820 --> 00:43:05,580
That is a really powerful way to look at it.

1146
00:43:05,580 --> 00:43:07,980
It's not just jargon, it's armor.

1147
00:43:07,980 --> 00:43:08,900
It is armor.

1148
00:43:08,900 --> 00:43:10,460
It protects you from being wrong,

1149
00:43:10,460 --> 00:43:12,980
from being blamed, and from being surprised.

1150
00:43:12,980 --> 00:43:15,740
My takeaway is simpler, but I think practical.

1151
00:43:15,740 --> 00:43:18,060
These words compress time.

1152
00:43:18,060 --> 00:43:20,500
Bandwidth is faster than saying,

1153
00:43:20,500 --> 00:43:21,540
I don't have enough time and energy

1154
00:43:21,540 --> 00:43:23,340
to do this task right now.

1155
00:43:23,340 --> 00:43:25,420
Deep dive is faster than saying,

1156
00:43:25,420 --> 00:43:27,460
let's look at this in extreme detail.

1157
00:43:27,460 --> 00:43:28,540
There's shortcuts.

1158
00:43:28,540 --> 00:43:30,820
Efficient compression, I like that.

1159
00:43:30,820 --> 00:43:33,620
So here's our final thought for you, the listener.

1160
00:43:33,620 --> 00:43:36,620
We've seen this relentless focus on avoiding surprises

1161
00:43:36,620 --> 00:43:39,800
and smoothness, but it raises a question.

1162
00:43:39,800 --> 00:43:43,160
Is this obsession of control actually stifling?

1163
00:43:43,160 --> 00:43:45,520
If we're always aligning and mitigating,

1164
00:43:45,520 --> 00:43:48,560
do we leave room for the happy accidents?

1165
00:43:48,560 --> 00:43:49,600
For creativity.

1166
00:43:49,600 --> 00:43:50,920
That is the trade-off, isn't it?

1167
00:43:50,920 --> 00:43:54,060
To use a business term, you trade chaos for control.

1168
00:43:54,060 --> 00:43:56,600
But sometimes chaos is where the innovation happens.

1169
00:43:56,600 --> 00:43:57,680
If you buffer everything,

1170
00:43:57,680 --> 00:43:59,200
you might buffer out the brilliance.

1171
00:43:59,200 --> 00:44:00,840
Something to mull over.

1172
00:44:00,840 --> 00:44:02,880
But for now, here's your call to action.

1173
00:44:02,880 --> 00:44:04,240
Pick one phrase from today.

1174
00:44:04,240 --> 00:44:05,640
Maybe it is surface blockers,

1175
00:44:05,640 --> 00:44:06,720
maybe it is quick whims,

1176
00:44:06,720 --> 00:44:08,600
maybe it is Q it for you.

1177
00:44:08,600 --> 00:44:09,600
Using your next meeting,

1178
00:44:09,600 --> 00:44:10,440
just drop it in and see

1179
00:44:10,440 --> 00:44:12,040
if it changes the energy in the room.

1180
00:44:12,040 --> 00:44:14,200
I bet it will make things smoother.

1181
00:44:14,200 --> 00:44:15,760
I see what you did there.

1182
